From 2384c8fd43def9bad86462a96620b5b8ad862ab5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: riotashley81-jpg <riotashley81@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:42:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 28/33] PGXP: cheaper per-instruction hooks

The ALU hook runs after every guest ALU instruction (about 7 million a
second in Vagrant Story's opening cutscene), and in cpu-mode it always ran
both tiers through several out-of-line helpers. In that cutscene the
emulation thread already sits near its frame budget, and the hooks pushed
heavy stretches over it: slowdown and audio underruns.

- ALU ops whose operands carry no shadow flag and no scalar, and the ops
  neither tier tracks (SLT, SLTI, ...), reset the destination directly;
  that is exactly what the two tiers produced for them.
- The scalar tier runs only when an operand could be precise.
- The small per-hook helpers are force-inlined (MSVC left pv_validate,
  gpr_scalar and others out of line); the render-pass journal keeps only
  its depth check inline.
- Source shadows are copied only when the destination aliases them.

Shadow results are unchanged: a randomized differential driver feeding
the old and new engine the same MIPS-consistent load/store/ALU/muldiv/
COP2 stream (4.8M steps, cpu-mode on and off) ends in identical state,
and test_pgxp passes. Cutscene benchmark (frames 120-2400 from the same
savestate, 3 rounds): emulation-thread cycles 130.7 -> 124.9 G; cpu-mode
off is 115.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
